// The `.` siblings — and the four different contracts hiding behind one suffix.
//
// `cargo xtask coverage` calls these "one mechanical paired test per base".
// They are not mechanical. Reading each family's own source turned up four
// distinct shapes, and a word written from its neighbour lands on the wrong
// one (F73):
//
//   operand 1    operand 2    result       family
//   ---------    ---------    ------       ------
//   workbench    stack        workbench    string.distance.   (D24's contract)
//   workbench    stack        stack        string.prefix.
//   workbench    workbench    workbench    head. tail. at.
//   workbench    (stack, n×)  workbench    pull.
//
// So every line below is written from the file that defines it, and the `take`
// after a word is present exactly when that family answers on the workbench.
//
// Not captured here:
//   pull. with more than one key — a multi-key MAP prints in an
//   unreproducible order (F76), so the stanza uses a single name.

// --- car. / cdr.: one workbench operand, answer on the workbench ---
[ 1 2 3 ] return car. take println
[ 1 2 3 ] return cdr. take println
[ "a" "b" ] return car. take println

// --- head. / tail. / at.: BOTH operands off the workbench ---
// The count goes on the workbench first, so it is underneath the list —
// `value_carcdr.rs:64-67` pulls the value, then the count, from the same side.
2 return [ 1 2 3 4 ] return head. take println
2 return [ 1 2 3 4 ] return tail. take println
2 return [ 1 2 3 4 ] return at. take println
1 return [ 9 8 7 ] return at. take println

// --- convert.to_*.: operand and answer both on the workbench, and the guard
// checks the workbench (unlike the print family below) ---
"42" return convert.to_int. take println
"3.5" return convert.to_float. take println
42 return convert.to_string. take println
1 return convert.to_bool. take println
"x" return convert.to_list. take println

// --- sort.: the plain mirror ---
[ 3 1 2 ] return sort. take println
[ "c" "a" "b" ] return sort. take println

// --- pull.: names off the workbench, values off the STACK, map to the
// workbench. One name only, per F76. ---
7 [ :only ] return pull. take println

// --- print. / println.: F77 — the guard reads the current stack while the
// value is pulled from the workbench, so a stack value must be present or the
// word refuses. The 0 below is that required-but-unread cell. ---
"printed-from-workbench" return 0 println.
"and-print" return 0 print. nl

// ============================================================================
// The rest of the `.` vocabulary. Two more contracts turn up here, so the four
// in the header are really six.
// ============================================================================

// --- get, / set, and their `.` siblings: the dictionary follows the side, the
// key and the value never do, and `get` splits its two answers across both
// (getsetinplace.rs:54-79). The dict is pulled FIRST, so it sits on top and
// the key goes underneath it. ---
:k dict :k 7 set get, println println
:k 9 dict :k 7 set set, :k get println
dict :k 7 set return :k get., println take println
dict :k 7 set return :k 9 set., take :k get println

// --- format.: only the TEMPLATE moves to the workbench. The values that fill
// it are still pulled from the stack and the result still lands there. ---
"world" "hello {a}" return format. println
5 3 "{x} and {y}" return format. println

// --- make.call. and merge. / ++. : plain mirrors, workbench in and out.
// merge. is D24's stated contract — receiver on the workbench, operand on the
// stack — which most of the `.` family does not actually follow. ---
:dup return make.call. take println
[ 3 ] [ 1 2 ] return merge. take println
[ 3 ] [ 1 2 ] return ++. take println

// --- the logic family: the LAMBDA always stays a stack operand. Only the
// condition, the count or the collection moves (if_fun.rs:27,31-34). ---
true return { "if. ran" println } if.
false return { "if. skipped" println } if.
true return { "?true. ran" println } ?true.
true return { "then" println } { "else" println } ?true*.
false return { "then" println } { "else" println } ifthenelse.
3 return { println } times.
[ 1 2 3 ] return { println } loop.
[ 1 2 3 ] return { 10 * } map. take println

// `while.` re-reads its condition from the WORKBENCH every iteration, so the
// body must leave the next one there. A body written for `while` does not
// port across (while_fun.rs:50-58).
true return { "tick" println false return } while.

// --- F78: `?false.` does NOT read the workbench. Its entry point passes
// StackOps::FromStack where its three siblings pass FromWorkBench
// (if_fun.rs:103-105 against :95-96), so it is `?false` with a different error
// prefix. The condition below is on the STACK, deliberately. ---
false { "?false. ran, from the stack" println } ?false.
